-> `expr ∈ container` used to require `container` to be a Set/Schema/
-> Union, erroring on anything else. But T26 already treats every value
-> as a set — a plain scalar like `n = 5` denotes the singleton set {5},
-> so `∈` against it means equality, not an error (T26 follow-up).
n = 5
assert 5 ∈ n
assert 1 ∉ n

-> Same rule for the statement-level narrowing form: `p ∈ { k = 1 }` (an
-> object literal with every field `=`, so it resolves as a plain Object,
-> not a Schema — see set_literal_and_value.code) narrows p to exactly
-> that object, the same as `p = { k = 1 }` would.
p ∈ { k = 1 }
assert p.k = 1
